# os/opensuse.capabilities — the openSUSE TUMBLEWEED capability declaration
# (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# Transcribed from core/PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager commands", openSUSE column.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/opensuse.capabilities
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THIS FILE IS TUMBLEWEED'S. LEAP'S IS os/opensuse.leap.capabilities.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ONE REPO SERVES TWO DISTRIBUTIONS THAT UPGRADE WITH DIFFERENT VERBS, and getting it
# wrong half-updates the box — it is the reason Core carried a
# `grep -qi tumbleweed /etc/os-release` probe in a portable module at all.
#
# A declaration is DATA: it cannot probe, so the choice has to be made somewhere. It
# is made in bootstrap.sh, which already reads /etc/os-release to decide which upgrade
# alias to advertise, and now relinks $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities to the Leap file on a
# non-Tumbleweed box. Tumbleweed is the default because it is what this repo primarily
# targets; a Leap box gets the other file, and a Leap→Tumbleweed migration is a
# re-bootstrap, which it already is for every other reason.
#
# TUMBLEWEED IS THE DEFAULT BUT THE FILES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. Two keys differ,
# and both differences are load-bearing — see PKG_UPGRADE and the scheduled-runner
# section below. Keep the rest in step by hand; there is deliberately no generator.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo zypper refresh
# `dup`, NOT `up` — THE FIRST OF THE TWO KEYS THAT DIFFER FROM THE LEAP FILE.
# Tumbleweed is rolling and upgrades as a distribution; `zypper up` on it holds
# packages back and leaves a box in a state neither dialect describes.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo zypper dup
PKG_INSTALL=sudo zypper in
PKG_REMOVE=sudo zypper rm
PKG_SEARCH=zypper se
PKG_OWNS=zypper se --provides
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=zypper -q list-updates

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y
# `dup` HAS NO MEANINGFUL PER-PACKAGE FORM, so a partial upgrade here is `up` — which
# is what a Tumbleweed `up -i` has always done. This is the one place the two dialects
# legitimately meet.
PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo zypper up
# NO PKG_UPGRADE_PRE: `dup` refreshes as part of the transaction.
# NO PKG_CLEANUP: zypper does not accumulate the way apt and brew do.

# ── how `up` counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `zypper list-updates` prints a PIPE-DELIMITED table: the `v` status column, then
# repository, then the package name in field 3. This is the only archive in the fleet
# whose output is not whitespace-separated.
PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^v[[:space:]]
PKG_PENDING_FS=|
PKG_PENDING_FIELD=3

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE — THE SECOND KEY THAT DIFFERS FROM THE LEAP FILE, and
# the reason the tier split is not only about spelling the verb.
#
# The flag drives PKG_UPGRADE, which here is `dup`. Declaring it would turn a nightly
# package update into an unattended DISTRIBUTION upgrade on a rolling distro — the
# exact hazard that keeps Arch and Gentoo from declaring it. That is also an ESCALATION
# from what this box does today: Core's untethered maint arm ran
# `zypper --non-interactive up` on any zypper box, never `dup`.
#
# Leap is versioned and upgrades with `up`, so it declares the flag. Apply with `up`.

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO TOOLS_OPTIN. PORTING-MATRIX.md's ²¹ cells show openSUSE PACKAGES jj and ast-grep,
# the two tools that are opt-in on Gentoo and the Debian family — so there is no delta
# from Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN default, and a declared list REPLACES that default
# rather than adding to it, so a copy here could only drift.
